  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1, paragraph surrounding equation (1.1)",
      "anchor": "q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ; subscripts denote partial derivatives",
      "observation": "The chapter introduces a vector-valued unknown depending on one spatial variable x and time t, with m components. Subscripts t and x indicate the corresponding partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "real eigenvalues and corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "For the constant-coefficient system (1.1), hyperbolicity means that A has real eigenvalues and a full set of m linearly independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3, Section 1.1, equation (1.8)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "The selected passage presents this PDE as the simplest example of a one-dimensional conservation law and identifies f(q) as the flux function."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3, equation (1.9) and following sentence",
      "anchor": "q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0; flux Jacobian matrix",
      "observation": "The source rewrites (1.8) in quasilinear form and relates hyperbolicity to the previously stated spectral conditions applied to the flux Jacobian."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "linear flux function f(q) = Aq; q, a vector of conserved quantities",
      "observation": "The linear system (1.1) is a conservation law with flux Aq. Nonlinear conservation laws have flux nonlinear in the vector q."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, Section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "d/dt ∫[x₁,x₂] q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))",
      "observation": "The integral form states that the amount between any two spatial points changes according to the flux at the endpoints. Each component of q measures the density of a conserved quantity."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "The source qualifies derivation of differential equation (1.8) from integral equation (1.10) by sufficient smoothness, and notes that relevant solutions can instead contain discontinuities."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Inherited notation: x ∈ ℝ is the single spatial variable and t ∈ ℝ is time; q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ is the unknown with m components. Equation (1.8) does not repeat these declarations or give explicit quantifiers. [Printed p. 1, paragraph surrounding (1.1); printed p. 3, (1.8).]",
      "f is the flux function of the state q. Its vector-valued interpretation and compatible input/output dimensions follow from the vector q, the addition in (1.8), and the Jacobian matrix in (1.9); an explicit function-type declaration for f is not supplied. [Printed p. 3, (1.8)–(1.9); printed p. 4, opening paragraph.]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The displayed equation concerns one spatial dimension and a homogeneous conservation law. [Printed p. 3, Section 1.1, introductory sentences and (1.8).]",
      "For a classical differential interpretation, the displayed partial derivatives must exist; this is an analyst inference from (1.8). The source explicitly requires sufficient smoothness of q and f(q) when deriving (1.8) from the integral form, without specifying a precise regularity class. [Printed p. 3, (1.8); printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph.]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The selected conservation-law PDE is q_t(x,t) + ∂_x[f(q(x,t))] = 0. This is a description of the equation's form, not an existence or uniqueness assertion about its solutions. [Printed p. 3, Section 1.1, introductory sentence and (1.8).]",
      "The function f(q) is the flux function associated with this conservation law. [Printed p. 3, sentence immediately following (1.8).]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives with respect to time and space. [Printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1).]",
      "The x subscript in f(q(x,t))_x differentiates the composed spatial flux at fixed t; it does not denote merely the derivative of f with respect to its state argument. This reading is supported by the rewrite f′(q)q_x. [Printed p. 3, (1.8)–(1.9).]",
      "For vector-valued q, the zero in (1.8) is the zero vector and the equation holds componentwise; this is an analyst inference from the inherited vector type and displayed sum. [Printed p. 1, type declaration following (1.1); printed p. 3, (1.8).]",
      "Each component of q represents the density of a conserved quantity, and endpoint fluxes account for changes in its spatial integral. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, (1.10) and following paragraph.]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The chapter studies time-dependent first-order systems, and Section 1.1 places conservation laws in the discussion of homogeneous hyperbolic equations. [Printed p. 1, introduction preceding (1.1); printed p. 3, Section 1.1 opening.]",
      "The nearby hyperbolicity criterion concerns the flux Jacobian f′(q): the previously stated matrix conditions are real eigenvalues and m linearly independent eigenvectors. These conditions describe hyperbolicity in the surrounding discussion; they are not additional terms in equation (1.8). [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph and sentence following (1.9).]",
      "Both linear flux f(q)=Aq and flux nonlinear in q occur within the conservation-law discussion. [Printed p. 4, opening paragraph.]",
      "The source distinguishes the differential form from an integral form and explicitly acknowledges discontinuous solutions for which the smooth derivation does not apply. [Printed p. 4, Sections 1.1.1–1.1.2.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Spatial dimension and state dimension",
        "details": [
          "One-dimensional refers to a single spatial coordinate, not necessarily to a scalar state: q inherits m components, and the continuation explicitly calls q a vector. [Printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1); printed p. 3, introduction to (1.8); printed p. 4, opening paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Flux dependence and homogeneity",
        "details": [
          "The displayed flux depends on x and t through q(x,t); no separate explicit spatial or temporal argument of f is displayed. [Printed p. 3, (1.8).]",
          "Equation (1.8) has zero right-hand side and no source term. The integral-form discussion separately mentions source terms when the relevant quantity is not conserved. [Printed p. 3, (1.8); printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, paragraph following (1.10).]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Differential and quasilinear forms",
        "details": [
          "The conservative form differentiates the flux composition in space; the adjacent quasilinear form is q_t+f′(q)q_x=0, where f′(q) is the flux Jacobian. [Printed p. 3, (1.8)–(1.9) and following sentence.]",
          "The source's smoothness qualification prevents treating the differential display as an unqualified classical equation for discontinuous solutions. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Scope of the selected claim",
        "details": [
          "The selected passage introduces a PDE form without prescribing initial data, boundary data, a solution-existence conclusion, or uniqueness. [Printed p. 3, Section 1.1, introduction to (1.8) and immediately following flux description.]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the conservative differential expression q_t(x,t)+∂_x[f(q(x,t))]=0, including its plus sign and zero right-hand side. [Printed p. 3, (1.8).]",
    "Preserve the distinction between differentiation in space of the flux composition and the state derivative f′ used in the quasilinear rewrite. [Printed p. 3, (1.8)–(1.9).]",
    "Permit a vector state with m components; the phrase one-dimensional does not restrict the state to a scalar. [Printed p. 1, type declaration after (1.1); printed p. 3, introduction to (1.8); printed p. 4, opening paragraph.]",
    "Identify f as the flux function and allow both linear and nonlinear fluxes. [Printed p. 3, sentence following (1.8); printed p. 4, opening paragraph.]",
    "Do not turn the displayed PDE form into an assertion that arbitrary functions q and f satisfy the equation. [Printed p. 3, introductory sentence identifying (1.8) as a conservation-law PDE.]",
    "Retain the source's smoothness qualification when asserting passage from the integral formulation to the differential formulation. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph.]"
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The precise regularity class and domain on which the classical PDE is imposed are not specified.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 1 gives q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ; printed p. 3, (1.8), supplies no domain restriction or regularity hypothesis; printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, says only that q and f(q) must be sufficiently smooth for the stated derivation.",
      "impact": "A precise classical formulation must choose differentiability assumptions and an evaluation domain without attributing a particular smoothness class or boundary convention to this passage."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The state domain of f and the admissible values of m are not explicitly declared in the selected passage.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 1 introduces m components; printed p. 3 names f(q) as the flux and f′(q) as a Jacobian; printed p. 4 calls q a vector of conserved quantities.",
      "impact": "Dimensional compatibility is supported, but a globally defined flux on all of ℝᵐ and a specific formal restriction on m are additional specification choices."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The exact statewise scope of the hyperbolicity requirement is not stated.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 3, after (1.9), relates hyperbolicity to the previously given matrix conditions on f′(q), without specifying a set of states over which those conditions hold.",
      "impact": "The surrounding discussion supports the spectral criterion but does not justify silently adding a precise universal state-domain hypothesis to the selected PDE description."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "Equation (1.8) presents a one-dimensional homogeneous conservation law: the time derivative of q plus the spatial derivative of its flux f(q) equals zero, with f identified as the flux function [printed p. 3, Section 1.1, (1.8) and following sentence]. The inherited unknown q is vector-valued, so one-dimensional concerns space rather than the number of conserved components [printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1); printed p. 4, opening paragraph]. This passage specifies the equation's form rather than claiming that arbitrary functions satisfy it or asserting existence and uniqueness [printed p. 3, introduction to (1.8)]. The source separately qualifies derivation of this differential form from the integral conservation law by sufficient smoothness and acknowledges discontinuous solutions [printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2]."
}
